          ],
          "content": "Exploring Mixtures and their Separation"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.953,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "N 73"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.089,
            0.562,
            0.111
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.1 How Can We Classify Mixtures?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.116,
            0.187,
            0.153
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.114,
            0.641,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "Grade 8 You have learnt that a mixture of sugar and CuriosityChapter 9 water has a uniform composition throughout. A well-stirred mixture of sugar and water is equally sweet in the first and the last sip. Such a mixture is called a **homogeneous mixture** (Fig. 5.1) or a **solution**. Other examples of homogeneous mixtures are vinegar (acetic acid in water), aerated drinks like soda (carbon dioxide in water), etc. A solution always remains homogeneous."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.685,
            0.089,
            0.907,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.683,
                0.268,
                0.908,
                0.284
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.1: Homogeneous mixture"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.269,
            0.641,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "On the other hand, a stirred mixture of sand and water is not uniform. The sand particles are easily visible in the water and settle with time. Such a mixture is called a **heterogeneous mixture** (Fig. 5.2). Is the mixture of oil and water homogeneous or heterogeneous? Can you think of some other heterogeneous mixtures?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.38,
            0.606,
            0.399
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.1: Let us experiment — Group activity"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.401,
            0.64,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "Divide the class into three groups — A, B and C. Each group prepares a mixture in a colourless, transparent glass tumbler or a 100 mL beaker by following these steps:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.681,
            0.292,
            0.905,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.455,
            0.641,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "1. Group A: Add one spatula of common salt to 50 mL of water in a beaker and stir it well. Label it A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.509,
            0.47,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "2. Group B: Add one spatula of chalk powder to 50 mL of water in a beaker and stir it well. Label it B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.564,
            0.471,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "3. Group C: Add a few drops of milk to 50 mL of water in a beaker and stir it well. Label it C."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.618,
            0.47,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": "4. Are the particles visible in each mixture? **Record** your observations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.672,
            0.47,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "5. Direct the light from a laser pointer through the beakers containing the mixtures (Fig. 5.3) and **observe** it from the side of the beaker in a direction perpendicular to the laser beam. Record your observations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.793,
            0.469,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "**Safety first**: Do not look directly into the laser beam. It can cause irreversible eye damage."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.844,
            0.469,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "6. **Predict** what you would observe in each of the beakers if you leave them undisturbed for a few minutes."
        },
        {
